  [34mNotification[39m  { id, taskId, [36mdueAt[39m: ISO, [36mfiredAt[39m: ISO, [36mreadAt[39m: ISO|[34mnull[39m, [36mdismissedAt[39m: ISO|[34mnull[39m }

  The important bit is [38;5;153mNotification.id[39m, which is [1mderived, not random[0m: [38;5;153m`${taskId}:${dueAt}`[39m. That single choice does most
  of the work in this design:

  - [1mFiring is idempotent by construction.[0m Appending is an upsert on that key, so a scheduler tick can run a thousand
    times and produce one notification. No "have I already fired this?" bookkeeping, no [38;5;153mlastTickAt[39m watermark to keep in
    sync.
  - [1mRe-scheduling re-arms correctly.[0m Edit a task's [38;5;153mdueAt[39m and the key changes, so it legitimately fires again at the new
    time — which is what you'd want, and which a random id or a task-keyed id would both get wrong.
  - [1mReload reconciliation is free.[0m The notification set is a pure function of [38;5;153m(tasks, now)[39m plus your read/dismiss state.
    Nothing needs to be replayed.

  [1mPersistence.[0m One key, [38;5;153mtasksapp/v1[39m, holding [38;5;153m{ version, tasks, notifications }[39m. Versioned so a later shape change
  migrates instead of exploding. Two failure modes handled explicitly, because localStorage has exactly two:

  - [1mCorrupt/unparseable[0m — don't throw away the user's data silently. Copy the raw string to [38;5;153mtasksapp/v1.corrupt[39m, start
    from empty, and show a one-line banner saying so.
  - [1mQuota exceeded[0m — drop dismissed notifications oldest-first, retry the write once, and if it still fails, surface the
    banner and keep running in memory. The app stays usable; it just stops persisting.

  [1mCascades.[0m Completing a task before its [38;5;153mdueAt[39m means it never fires. Completing it after doesn't retract the
  notification you already got — that happened. Deleting a task dismisses its notifications, since the thing they point
  at is gone.
